   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.2",
    "topic_title": "Sine, Cosine, and Tangent",
    "stem": "The terminal ray of angle θ intersects the unit circle at (−3/5, 4/5). What is tan θ?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "−4/3",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "On the unit circle, cos θ = −3/5 and sin θ = 4/5. Since cosine is nonzero, tan θ = sin θ/cos θ = (4/5)/(−3/5) = −4/3."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "−3/4",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reciprocal-ratio error: dividing cosine by sine gives −3/4. Tangent requires sine divided by cosine, not the reverse. Fix: Write tan θ = y/x before substituting the coordinates."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "4/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sign-loss error: using only the coordinate magnitudes gives 4/3. Here sine is positive and cosine is negative, so their quotient is negative. Fix: Keep each coordinate’s sign when forming the quotient."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "4/5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sine-for-tangent error: the y-coordinate, 4/5, gives sin θ, not tan θ. Tangent is the ratio of the y-coordinate to the x-coordinate. Fix: Divide the y-coordinate by the x-coordinate after checking that x ≠ 0."
     }
    ]
   },
